    "done_when": "One public entry in a new module, imas_ambix/gs/description_artifact.py, authors two JT-60SA machine artifacts, one for each operating phase, from /home/ITER/mcintos/Code/imas-ambix/maps/jt-60sa/machine_description/OP1 and OP2. For each phase it reads that store's five IDSs (magnetics, pf_active, pf_passive, tf, wall) through imas and writes them into an IMAS HDF5 entry (imas:hdf5?path=) in a staging directory of its own, the form imas_ambix/gs/artifact_geometry.py MachineArtifactGeometryReader opens, as nova's nova/imas/mast_geometry.py write_and_reopen writes it; the netCDF files and the converter receipt.json are never artifact members. It derives the identity create_machine_artifact_manifest takes rather than accepting it as input: one registry digest from a JT-60SA geometry registry built over both phases; each phase's physical digest from that phase's geometry content (not file bytes, so library write metadata does not move it); each phase's shot range from that phase's first_shot and last_shot in the maps list of /home/ITER/mcintos/Code/imas-ambix/maps/jt-60sa/machine_map.json; and complete with any unresolved gaps named. It calls create_machine_artifact_manifest and materialize_machine_artifact for each phase into a cache directory it is given, and resolve_machine_artifact verifies each. Read §7 of the plan and docs/evidence/fragments/jt60sa-machine-map/jtmm-artifact-authoring-route.html first: the fragment maps nova's generic entries and the MAST and DIII-D producers this follows. Nothing is added to nova, nothing under maps/ is written, and the durable cache route, the pin table and the selector change are left to jtmm-machine-keyed-selection. New tests in tests/gs/test_description_artifact.py show five things: authoring twice gives each phase the same semantic identity and physical digest; the OP1 and OP2 artifacts carry different physical digests, one shared registry digest and disjoint shot ranges; moving one PF coil position in a tmp_path copy of one phase's store changes that phase's physical digest; each artifact resolves with its own expected digests; and each artifact directory reopens through imas.DBEntry imas:hdf5 at the manifest's dictionary version with pf_active, pf_passive, wall and magnetics present. On the fleet allocation (SLURM_JOB_ID set), with the worktree's maps a symlink to the main checkout's maps/, run in the foreground and capture once, to a log in the run directory whose first lines give the head revision and command and whose last line is EXIT=: uv run --no-sync pytest -p no:cacheprovider tests/gs/test_description_artifact.py; it exits 0, and uv run --no-sync ruff check imas_ambix tests exits 0. The fragment quotes, for each phase, the authored artifact's semantic identity, physical digest, registry digest, shot range and OCI tag. A landing fragment is written to the fragment path.",
